    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mao rose from a peasant family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> to become a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, built its Red Army on the peasantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, and took the party's leadership during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a> of 1934-1935.</li><li>After defeating the Nationalist government he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1949 and led land reform, industrialisation and a series of campaigns against those the party named its enemies.</li><li>Under his rule tens of millions died, most in the famine that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> of 1958-1962, and millions were persecuted in the Cultural Revolution; he is also credited with gains in literacy, women's status, health and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s standing as a power.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In his first article in the radical magazine New Youth, published in April 1917, Mao urged readers to increase their physical strength to serve the revolution; his earliest poem to survive, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>&quot;, was written on the eve of the Autumn Harvest Uprising of 1927.</p></aside>
    </section>

