    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1893</span><span class="rf-text">Mao Zedong is born near Shaoshan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1921</span><span class="rf-text">He is one of 13 delegates at the first congress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1934</span><span class="rf-text">The Red Army breaks out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> Soviet and begins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1935</span><span class="rf-text">At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zunyi">Zunyi</a> conference Mao becomes de facto leader of the party and the Red Army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text">He proclaims the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1958</span><span class="rf-text">He launches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-chinese-famine">Great Chinese Famine</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1966</span><span class="rf-text">He launches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1976</span><span class="rf-text">Mao dies, aged 82, and is succeeded at first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-guofeng">Hua Guofeng</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen.jpg" alt="Sun Yat-sen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%AD%99%E4%B8%AD%E5%B1%B1%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">上海波尔照相馆, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mao Zedong was born on 26 December 1893 near Shaoshan village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. His father, Mao Yichang, was a formerly impoverished peasant who had become one of the wealthiest farmers in Shaoshan; Mao described him as a stern disciplinarian who beat his children. His mother, Wen Qimei, was a devout Buddhist. At eight he was sent to the village school, where he disliked the Confucian classics and preferred novels such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a> and Water Margin. At 13 his father married him to Luo Yixiu, aged 17, to unite their land-owning families; Mao refused to recognise her as his wife and became a fierce critic of arranged marriage.</p><p>On his father's farm he read voraciously, including translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and Darwin. In 1911 he began secondary school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>, where he read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>'s newspaper and cut off his queue. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> broke out he joined the rebel army as a private, without fighting, and left it in 1912. He then trained as a teacher at the Fourth Normal School of Changsha, where his professor Yang Changji introduced him to Chen Duxiu's magazine New Youth. He was elected secretary of the Students' Society in 1915, helped found the Renovation of the People Study Society in April 1918, and graduated in June 1919.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mao Zedong&#8221; cites where it covers Youth and the Xinhai Revolution: 1893–1911, Fourth Normal School of Changsha: 1912–1919, Beijing, anarchism, and Marxism: 1917–1919, New Culture and political protests: 1919–1920:</p><ul><li>Rebecca E. Karl, Mao Zedong and China in the twentieth-century world : a concise history (Duke University Press, 2010)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing<span>Married</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek.jpg" alt="Chiang Kai-shek" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiang_Kai-shek%EF%BC%88%E8%94%A3%E4%B8%AD%E6%AD%A3%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> Yang Changji found him a post as assistant to the university librarian Li Dazhao, who added Marxism to the doctrines of the Chinese revolutionary movement; Mao, influenced also by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin">Kropotkin</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>, moved towards Marxism over the winter of 1919. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> he organised students and workers against the governor Zhang Jingyao and edited radical magazines. In 1921 he was one of 13 delegates at the first congress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> (CCP) and became its secretary for Hunan. When the party allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> (KMT), he rose to become the KMT's propaganda chief, and in 1924 a visit home convinced him of the revolutionary potential of the peasantry.</p><p>In 1927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> turned on the Communists in the White Terror, and the CCP lost about 15,000 of its 25,000 members. Mao led the failed Autumn Harvest Uprising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> and retreated with 1,000 survivors to the Jinggang Mountains, where he built a base, confiscated landlords' land and moulded his men, among them former bandits, into a disciplined force. Party leaders who held that only the urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> could lead a revolution expelled him and ridiculed his peasant strategy. In 1934 the Red Army broke out of the encircled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> Soviet on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>; at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zunyi">Zunyi</a> in January 1935 Mao became de facto leader of party and army, with Stalin's support. Only 7,000-8,000 of those who set out reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution.jpg" alt="Cultural Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1967-02_1967%E5%B9%B4%E7%9A%84%E7%BA%A2%E5%8D%AB%E5%85%B5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">China Pictorial, 1967, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a> Mao proposed an alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian-incident">Xi'an Incident</a> a United Front was formed with the KMT. The Red Army grew from 50,000 to 500,000, and Mao wrote Protracted Warfare and On New Democracy. When the civil war resumed after Japan's surrender in 1945, his forces defeated the Nationalist government, which withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, and on 1 October 1949 he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a>.</p><p>In October 1950 he sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-volunteer-army">People's Volunteer Army</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, in which at least 180,000 Chinese troops died. At home the party redistributed land and suppressed "counter-revolutionaries", and the government is credited with eradicating opium in the 1950s. The first five-year plan (1953-1958) brought rapid growth in heavy industry with Soviet help. Under the Two Bombs, One Satellite programme <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> built atomic and hydrogen bombs and became the fifth state with nuclear weapons, and from 1964 Mao pushed the Third Front, the building of industry in the interior.</p><p>In January 1958 he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> to make China an industrial nation, merging collectives into vast people's communes. In 1966, fearing that a new elite had replaced the old and that the revolution was losing its zeal, he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> to keep China in a state of "continuous revolution", with himself at the centre of a cult of personality.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai.jpg" alt="Peng Dehuai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai">Peng Dehuai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Peng_Dehuai_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">​《新华社》记者 郑景康, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mao's rule was marked by mass killing and persecution. In the Futian incident of 1930 his loyalists tortured many and executed between 2,000 and 3,000 dissenters. In the land reform, people accused of being "landlords" and "rich peasants" were beaten to death at mass meetings; Mao claimed that 700,000 "counter-revolutionaries" were killed in 1950-1952, while estimates of the deaths range between 2 million and 5 million, and at least 1.5 million were sent to labour camps. He defended the killings as necessary to secure power. When critics spoke out in the Hundred Flowers Campaign, the Anti-Rightist Movement persecuted at least 550,000 people, mostly intellectuals.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> cadres exaggerated harvests and requisitioned grain that did not exist, and the famine of 1959-1962 directly caused the death of some 30 million peasants. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai">Peng Dehuai</a> criticised the policy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lu">Lushan</a> in 1959, Mao purged him; the CCP later concluded that as many as six million were wrongly punished in that campaign. Bernstein concludes that Mao "wilfully ignored the lessons" of the first phase. In the Cultural Revolution, Maurice Meisner gives about 400,000 deaths as a widely accepted minimum, and MacFarquhar and Schoenhals put those killed in rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> alone at 750,000 to 1.5 million. Li Rui, once on his secretarial staff, said that Mao "put no value on human life".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lin-biao.jpg" alt="Lin Biao" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linbiao2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mao stepped down as President in April 1959, keeping the chairmanship of the party and of the Military Commission, and in 1962 abandoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi">Liu Shaoqi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> rescued the economy by disbanding the communes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> destroyed much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s cultural heritage and drove Liu Shaoqi among others to his death. Mao chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a> as his successor, but a divide opened between them, and on 13 September 1971 Lin died in a plane crash over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, presumably fleeing China; the CCP declared that he had planned to depose Mao. Mao declared the Cultural Revolution over in 1969, though many historians date its end to 1976. After President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a>'s visit in 1972 relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> improved.</p><p>His health declined in his last years, probably aggravated by chain-smoking, and his lung and heart ailments were kept a state secret. After heart attacks on 11 May, 26 June and 2 September 1976 he died shortly after midnight on 9 September, aged 82. His embalmed body lay in state in the Great Hall of the People for a week, and on 18 September a three-minute silence was observed across China. Despite his request to be cremated, his body was placed on display in the Mausoleum of Mao Zedong. He was succeeded first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-guofeng">Hua Guofeng</a>, then in 1978 by Deng Xiaoping.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mao Zedong&#8221; cites where it covers Death and aftermath:</p><ul><li>Rebecca E. Karl, Mao Zedong and China in the Twentieth-Century World: A Concise History (Duke University Press, 2010)</li><li>Jonathan Spence, Mao Zedong (Viking Press, 1999)</li><li>Xing Lu, The Rhetoric of Mao Zedong: Transforming China and Its People (University of South Carolina Press, 2017)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping.jpg" alt="Deng Xiaoping" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deng_Xiaoping_at_the_arrival_ceremony_for_the_Vice_Premier_of_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mao has been called one of the most important and influential individuals of the 20th century. He is credited with ending decades of civil war, improving the status of women, literacy and education, a near doubling of life expectancy and the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s industry, and under him the People's Republic took China's permanent seat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a>. His China has also been described as a totalitarian regime responsible for mass repression, and he as one of the great tyrants of the century. His theory of people's war still guides insurgents, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoism">Maoism</a> influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>.</p><p>In 1981 the CCP's Resolution on party history held that his contributions far outweighed his mistakes, which it dated mainly from 1965; a common expression, first attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>, is that Mao was 70 percent right and 30 percent wrong. Chen Yun remarked that had Mao died in 1956 his achievements would have been immortal. In the West, Philip Short argued that a bit more than half of the deaths under Mao were unintended consequences of famine; Alexander Pantsov and Steven Levine called him "a successful creator and ultimately an evil destroyer"; Stuart Schram agreed with the Chinese view that his merits outweighed his faults while asking how land for peasants could be weighed against millions executed; and Maurice Meisner wrote that the era would still be recorded as one of the great modernising epochs in world history.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mao Zedong&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy, Assessment in China, Assessment in Western world, Third World:</p><ul><li>Rebecca E. Karl, Mao Zedong and China in the Twentieth-Century World: a Concise History (Duke University Press, 2010)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">On the globe, stop by stop</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/journeys?j=long-march">The Long March of the First Front Army<span>Journey</span></a></p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers<span>1935</span></a><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red<span>1942</span></a><a href="/songs/without-the-communist-party-there-would-be-no-new-china">Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New China<span>c. 1943</span></a><a href="/songs/red-sun-in-the-sky">Red Sun in the Sky<span>1949</span></a><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland<span>1950</span></a><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman<span>1964</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mao Zedong, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> in 1893, helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, led it on the Long March and in 1949 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. His campaigns, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> cost tens of millions of lives, while he is also credited with ending civil war and with gains in literacy, health and national power.</em></p>

